      "id": 1,
      "text": "East Hazel Crest began as a farming community called South Harvey in 1870, where early settlers cultivated the land that would later become the village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofhazelcrest.org/community/history.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1890, newspaper editor William McClintock moved from Ohio, bought 80 acres, and built a depot that turned the area into a transportation hub for Chicago.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofhazelcrest.org/community/history.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village adopted the name Hazel Crest in 1900, inspired by the hazelnut bushes that grew on a rise just south of town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofhazelcrest.org/community/history.php",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "East Hazel Crest officially incorporated as a village in 1912, solidifying its community structure and local governance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofhazelcrest.org/community/history.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today, the Metra station that sits where the original depot once stood connects residents north to Millennium station and south to University Park station.",
